ML_PAGE("l7-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l7\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 7 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the scenario, then evaluate and conclude. Each mock question below has its full model answer behind a reveal; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l7\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The four-move answer (10-20 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Choose and state the formula</b><p>Name the ratio the question needs and its convention: ending or average balances, sales or credit sales, long-term debt or total liabilities.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Calculate from the question's data</b><p>Show the working with units: \"times\" for ratios, ringgit for working capital, per-share figures for EPS, DPS and book value.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Interpret the mechanism</b><p>Explain <em>what caused</em> the number (faster-growing liabilities, slower collections, more leverage) and what it means for future cash flows and risk.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Qualify and conclude</b><p>Name what is missing (peers, maturities, cash-flow detail, price) and give a conditional judgment that new evidence could change.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Strong Chapter 7 answers follow the chain: economic conditions → industry exposure → company operations and financing → future cash flows and risk → estimated value compared with the market price. A ratio is evidence for that chain, never a stock recommendation on its own.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p><b>Q:</b> Apex's current ratio moved from $2.00$ to $1.56$. Interpret the change.</p>\n        <p>\"The current ratio fell, so the company is a bad investment.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">No working, no cause, no qualification, and it jumps from one liquidity ratio straight to an investment verdict.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"The current ratio fell from $\\dfrac{80}{40} = 2.00$ to $\\dfrac{125}{80} = 1.56$ because current liabilities grew faster than current assets, even though working capital rose from $40$ to $45$. The short-term liquidity cushion is therefore smaller relative to obligations, and much of the current assets are receivables and inventory that may not convert to cash promptly. However, before judging whether Apex faces a financing problem, or whether the share price is attractive, we should examine the maturity of its liabilities, its cash collections, its available credit facilities and industry practice.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Formula + working + cause + qualification + a conditional link to the investment decision.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Mock examination: questions with model answers</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><span>From economic news to investment analysis (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A central bank raises interest rates while consumer spending slows. Horizon manufactures expensive industrial machinery, finances inventories partly with floating-rate borrowing and exports half its output. A broker says, \"The economy is weak, so Horizon's share price must fall next month.\"</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Explain the top-down security-analysis process and identify one question to investigate at each level.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Analyse two channels through which the scenario could affect Horizon's future cash flows or required return.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Evaluate the broker's prediction, including the difference between actual news and what investors already expected.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Explain how industry competition and Horizon's individual financial position could lead to a different conclusion from an economy-only analysis.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Top-down analysis [5].</strong> Economic analysis asks how growth, rates, inflation and exchange rates may affect firms. For Horizon, ask whether higher financing costs and slower customer spending are likely to reduce machinery demand. Industry analysis asks about machinery-sector competition, customer bargaining power, regulation and stage of growth. Company analysis examines Horizon's order backlog, margins, liquidity, debt terms and cash generation. These findings inform cash-flow and risk assumptions used to estimate value and compare it with the share price. <em>Marking: economy 1, industry 1, company 1, relevant questions 1, valuation connection 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Transmission [5].</strong> Higher rates can raise interest expense on floating-rate borrowing, lowering cash available to equity holders if all else is equal. Slower spending can prompt customers to delay major equipment purchases, reducing Horizon's volume and potentially margins. Exchange-rate movements could alter export revenue when translated into home currency and affect competitiveness, but the direction depends on currencies, pricing and costs. Rates may also alter the required return used to discount expected cash flows. <em>Marking: 2 marks for each of two explained channels and 1 for their valuation relevance or qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Prediction [5].</strong> The claim is too certain. Stock prices reflect investors' expectations and may adjust before economic weakness appears in reported data. If weakness is already priced in, or actual results are less bad than expected, the share could rise; worse-than-expected news could cause it to fall. Uncertainty, other news and changing required returns also matter. A weak economy alone does not establish next month's direction. <em>Marking: conditional judgment 1, expectations versus surprise 2, possible outcomes 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Industry and company [5].</strong> A firm with long contracts, a strong balance sheet and pricing power may withstand a slowdown better than a highly leveraged competitor with weak orders. Conversely, intense competition or customer concentration may worsen the effect. Compare margins, backlog, refinancing schedule, debt service and competitors before interpreting the economy-only forecast. An attractive business can still be a poor investment at an excessive price. <em>Marking: industry mechanism 2, company financial mechanism 2, price/valuation qualification 1.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><span>Reading financial statements together (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>The following simplified information describes Apex. Its accounts balance; there are no preferred shares. Figures are RM million.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Beginning of year</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">End of year</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">20</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Receivables</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inventory</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other current assets</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-current assets</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">120</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">115</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total assets</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>200</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>240</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Current liabilities</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">40</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-current liabilities</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total liabilities</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>120</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>160</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinary equity</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Annual sales were 300, cost of goods sold 180, EBIT 40, interest expense 10, tax 6, and net income 24. Cash flow from operations was 10. Assume all sales were on credit.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Distinguish the balance sheet, income statement and cash-flow statement by what each reports and its time frame. Verify the ending accounting equation.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate the beginning and ending current ratios and working capital; interpret their movement.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain why RM24 profit can coexist with RM10 operating cash flow. Use changes in working capital as a possible mechanism without claiming they fully reconcile the difference.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Identify two concerns and two follow-up checks before deciding that Apex is in financial difficulty.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Statements [5].</strong> The balance sheet reports assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time; the income statement reports revenues, expenses and profit over a period; the cash-flow statement reports cash generated or used in operating, investing and financing activities over a period. At year-end, $240=160+80$, so assets equal liabilities plus equity. <em>Marking: 1 each per statement, 1 for the time distinction, 1 for the equation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Liquidity [5].</strong> Beginning current assets are $20+30+30=80$; ending current assets are $15+50+60=125$.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Beginning current ratio}=80/40=2.00,\\quad \\text{ending current ratio}=125/80=1.5625\\approx1.56$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Beginning working capital}=80-40=40,\\quad \\text{ending working capital}=125-80=45$$</div>\n<p>The ratio fell despite an increase in working capital because current liabilities grew proportionately faster. There is a smaller relative short-term cushion; current assets include receivables and inventory that may not convert to cash promptly. <em>Marking: 2 ratios 2, working-capital figures 2, interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Profit versus cash [5].</strong> Net income is accrual-based, whereas operating cash flow records cash effects of operating activity. Sales booked on credit can increase receivables without immediate cash receipt; buying or producing additional inventory can absorb cash before sale. Noncash expenses, timing of payables and other working-capital changes can also affect the reconciliation. The stated data do not identify every reconciliation item, so they do not prove a particular cause for the full RM14 difference. <em>Marking: accrual versus cash 2, working-capital channels 2, limitation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Concerns and follow-up [5].</strong> Receivables rose by RM20 and inventory by RM30 while cash declined; the liquidity ratio also fell and operating cash flow is below profit. Investigate aged receivables and subsequent collections, inventory composition and obsolescence, liability due dates, credit facilities and seasonality, and the full cash-flow reconciliation. Growth or planned stocking may partly explain the changes. None alone proves distress. <em>Marking: 2 concerns 2, 2 relevant checks 2, balanced judgment 1.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><span>Asset efficiency and liquidity (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Use Apex's figures from Question 2. A competitor's comparable annual receivables turnover is 10 times and inventory turnover is 6 times. Assume accounting policies and periods are comparable for this exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate Apex's receivables turnover, approximate average collection period using 365 days, and inventory turnover.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Calculate total asset turnover and explain the economic meaning of each turnover measure.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Compare receivables and inventory turnover with the competitor. Explain two plausible causes or consequences of the differences.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate the claim: \"A higher inventory turnover is always better.\" Identify information that could change the conclusion.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Receivables and inventory [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Average receivables}=(30+50)/2=40;\\quad \\text{turnover}=300/40=7.50\\text{ times}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Approximate collection period}=365/7.50=48.67\\text{ days}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Average inventory}=(30+60)/2=45;\\quad \\text{inventory turnover}=180/45=4.00\\text{ times}$$</div>\n<p>The collection estimate is an approximation; actual payment timing and seasonality may differ. <em>Marking: averages 2, turnovers 2, days 1, interpretation or units 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Asset turnover [4].</strong> Average assets are $(200+240)/2=220$ and total asset turnover is $300/220=1.36$ times. Receivables turnover relates credit sales to average outstanding customer balances; inventory turnover relates cost of sales to average inventory; asset turnover relates sales to the asset base. These are different activities and should not be interchanged. <em>Marking: calculation 2, meanings 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Comparison [5].</strong> Apex collects receivables about 7.5 times per year compared with the peer's 10 and turns inventory 4 times versus 6. Slower collection could mean looser credit, delayed customers or different seasonality; slower inventory turnover might reflect higher stocks, weak sales, preparation for growth or different product mix. Either can tie up cash and require funding. Examine aged balances, stock condition and comparable accounting before attributing a cause. <em>Marking: comparison 2, two causal explanations/consequences 2, qualification 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [5].</strong> Reject \"always.\" Excessively lean inventory may create stockouts, lost sales and supply-chain disruption; a high ratio can also reflect different purchasing practices or a temporarily small average denominator. Examine service levels, shortages, inventory quality, the product mix and peer definitions. A slower rate may be justified by a deliberate inventory buffer, although obsolescence can make it risky. <em>Marking: conclusion 1, trade-off 2, relevant evidence 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><span>Leverage and profitability (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Continue with Apex's data. Recall EBIT = 40, interest expense = 10, sales = 300 and net income = 24. Total liabilities at year-end are 160 and ordinary equity is 80; beginning and ending total assets are 200 and 240, and ordinary equity is 80 at both dates.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [4] Calculate the year-end liabilities-to-equity ratio and times interest earned. Explain what each indicates.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [6] Calculate net profit margin, return on average assets and return on average equity.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Decompose ROE into net profit margin x asset turnover x equity multiplier using consistently averaged balances. Explain what leverage contributes to this identity.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate: \"A higher ROE proves management improved operations.\" Include a stress scenario and identify at least two additional checks.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Leverage [4].</strong> Ending liabilities-to-equity $=160/80=2.00$ times. Times interest earned $=EBIT/\\text{interest}=40/10=4.00$ times. The first compares liabilities with equity under the stated convention; the second indicates EBIT is four times the current period's interest expense. Neither guarantees future solvency, and EBIT is not a direct cash-flow measure. <em>Marking: 1 per calculation, 1 per interpretation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Profitability [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net margin}=24/300=8.00\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ROA=24/[(200+240)/2]=24/220=10.91\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ROE=24/[(80+80)/2]=24/80=30.00\\%$$</div>\n<p>These show net earnings per unit of sales, average assets and average ordinary equity respectively. <em>Marking: 2 marks each for correct formula, result and meaning.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Decomposition [5].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ROE=(24/300)\\times(300/220)\\times(220/80)=0.08\\times1.3636\\times2.75=30\\%$$</div>\n<p>The equity multiplier, 2.75, relates average assets to average equity. It captures how much assets are supported per unit of equity in this identity; financing and liabilities contribute to the gap. A higher multiplier can amplify equity returns, but debt cost and the effect of leverage on net income must also be considered. <em>Marking: components 3, identity 1, leverage interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [5].</strong> Higher ROE can arise from higher margin or turnover, but also from a higher equity multiplier or a smaller equity base. For instance, if EBIT falls to RM20 while interest remains RM10, coverage drops from 4 to 2 times before any further deterioration. Investigate margin and asset-turnover trends, debt maturity and interest terms, operating cash flow, and peers. Improved ROE does not establish improved operations or adequate compensation for increased risk. <em>Marking: counterargument 2, stress illustration 1, checks 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><span>Per-share measures and valuation signals (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Beacon has annual net income of RM25 million, preferred dividends of RM1 million, 12 million weighted-average ordinary shares, annual ordinary dividends of RM9.6 million, and ordinary book equity of RM96 million. Its current share price is RM30. For this exercise, assume the 12 million weighted-average shares also equal shares outstanding at the balance-sheet date. A forecast suggests earnings growth of 10% per year; compute PEG by entering growth as <strong>10</strong>, not 0.10.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate EPS, dividend per ordinary share and payout ratio.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [6] Calculate P/E, book value per ordinary share and P/B.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [3] Calculate PEG under the stated convention and identify one limitation.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate the claim: \"P/E above 10 and P/B above 1 prove the share is overpriced.\" Identify the additional evidence required before a recommendation.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Earnings and distributions [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EPS=(25-1)/12=RM2.00$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$DPS=9.6/12=RM0.80;\\quad \\text{payout}=0.80/2.00=40\\%$$</div>\n<p>Preferred dividends are deducted because EPS measures income available to ordinary shareholders. <em>Marking: 2 each for EPS, DPS and payout.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Price measures [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P/E=30/2=15.00\\text{ times}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Book value/share}=96/12=RM8.00;\\quad P/B=30/8=3.75\\text{ times}$$</div>\n<p>P/E compares the share price to earnings per share for the specified period; P/B compares price with accounting equity per share. Neither ratio alone determines intrinsic value. <em>Marking: 2 each for correct calculation and meaning.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) PEG [3].</strong> $PEG=15/10=1.50$ under the explicitly stated percentage-point convention. It is sensitive to the growth estimate and the consistency of earnings and growth periods; a mechanical threshold is not proof of fair value. <em>Marking: computation 2, limitation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [5].</strong> Reject the claim. High multiples can reflect expected growth, risk, return on capital or accounting differences; low multiples can reflect poor prospects or risk. Investigate earnings quality, sustainable cash flows, expected growth, leverage, peer and industry comparability, book-value composition and the required return, then estimate value under transparent assumptions. A share can trade above book value without being overpriced. <em>Marking: conclusion 1, reasons multiples differ 2, additional evidence and valuation 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><span>Integrated decision and research plan (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Apex's shares trade at RM12; its estimated intrinsic value is RM15 per share, calculated using assumptions supplied by an analyst. Apex's sales grew 5% in the latest year while year-end receivables rose from RM30 million to RM50 million and inventory from RM30 million to RM60 million. Operating cash flow was RM10 million versus RM24 million of net income. Management proposes extra borrowing to fund expansion.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the percentage growth in receivables and inventory; compare both with sales growth.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Analyse what this pattern and the operating-cash-flow gap might indicate. Give plausible alternative explanations.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Evaluate the alleged undervaluation and explain how leverage and the valuation assumptions affect the conclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a prioritized research plan covering the economy, industry, financial statements and valuation. State a conditional decision and what new evidence would change it.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<hr /><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Growth [5].</strong> Receivables growth $=(50-30)/30=66.67\\%$. Inventory growth $=(60-30)/30=100.00\\%$. Both substantially exceed 5% sales growth, indicating more working capital tied up relative to the stated sales trend. <em>Marking: 2 marks each for calculations, 1 for comparison.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Interpretation [5].</strong> Slow collection or expanding credit may explain receivables; inventory may be accumulating because of weak demand or stocking in anticipation of growth. These increases can absorb cash and help explain operating cash flow below net income. However, a deliberate expansion, seasonality or timing of cash collections and payments could also contribute. Obtain aging schedules, subsequent collections, stock aging, credit terms and the detailed cash-flow reconciliation; the pattern is a warning, not proof of manipulation or distress. <em>Marking: mechanisms 2, cash link 1, alternatives and checks 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Value and financing [5].</strong> RM15 estimated value is RM3 above the RM12 price, or $3/12=25\\%$ upside to that estimate before costs; it is an estimate, not a guaranteed gain. Check the analyst's cash-flow growth, working-capital needs, discount rate and financing assumptions. Borrowing may fund profitable expansion, but interest and repayment commitments increase financial risk and can change both expected cash flows and required return. If slower cash conversion persists or borrowing costs increase, the estimated value may fall below price. <em>Marking: comparison and calculation 2, estimate limitation 1, leverage and assumptions 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Research plan and conditional decision [5].</strong> First assess macroeconomic demand and rates relevant to Apex, then industry growth, competition and customer conditions. Review audited statements, receivables and inventory aging, operating cash conversion, borrowing terms and peer trends; reconstruct a valuation under base and adverse scenarios. Defer a firm buy recommendation until the working-capital increases and financing are explained and a conservative estimate still justifies the price for the investor's risk constraints. Strong subsequent collections and viable project returns could support buying; worsening collections or refinancing risk could justify avoiding it. <em>Marking: economic and industry checks 1, financial checks 1, valuation sensitivity 1, conditional decision 1, change-of-evidence trigger 1.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l7\" id=\"l7s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">5 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"From numbers to a defensible investment judgment\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s1\" title=\"From numbers to a defensible investment judgment\">From ratios to a defensible judgment</button></li><li data-full=\"Mock subjective examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s2\" title=\"Mock subjective examination\">Mock exam: six 20-mark questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Full model answers and illustrative marking guidance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s3\" title=\"Full model answers and illustrative marking guidance\">Model answers and marking guidance</button></li><li data-full=\"Short-answer revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s4\" title=\"Short-answer revision\">Short-answer revision</button></li><li data-full=\"Formula-selection map\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7s-s5\" title=\"Formula-selection map\">Formula-selection map</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l7\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Chapter 7 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>5 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 7 - Subjective Exam Preparation</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">From numbers to a defensible investment judgment</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Security analysis requires you to move through a chain: <strong>economic conditions → industry exposure → company operations and financing → future cash-flow expectations → estimated value compared with market price</strong>. A historical financial ratio is evidence for that chain; it is not, by itself, a stock recommendation.</p>\n<p>A weak answer says, \"The current ratio fell, so the company is a bad investment.\" A stronger answer says, \"The current ratio fell from 2.0 to 1.56 because current liabilities grew faster than current assets. The short-term liquidity cushion is smaller; however, we should examine maturity dates, cash collection, available financing and industry practice before judging whether the firm faces a financing problem or whether the share price is attractive.\"</p>\n<p>Answer using the question's data, identify the appropriate formula and denominator, calculate, interpret, and explain a relevant qualification. The following original questions, model answers and mark allocations are <strong>practice guidance</strong>, not an official exam or marking scheme. Do the questions before reading the separate model answers.</p>\n<h4>Conventions for this practice paper</h4>\n<p>Unless a question says otherwise: all currency amounts are RM million; use the beginning-and-ending average for asset, equity, receivable and inventory turnover or return calculations; use ending balances for current ratio, working capital, debt/equity and book value per share; and round to two decimal places. Credit sales equal total sales only where stated. Define debt/equity specifically as <strong>total liabilities ÷ ordinary shareholders' equity</strong> in these exercises; other analysts may use interest-bearing debt or other definitions. Annual ratios use one full financial year. These conventions make the calculations comparable rather than implying that every source uses the same formula.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Mock subjective examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 1 - From economic news to investment analysis (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A central bank raises interest rates while consumer spending slows. Horizon manufactures expensive industrial machinery, finances inventories partly with floating-rate borrowing and exports half its output. A broker says, \"The economy is weak, so Horizon's share price must fall next month.\"</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Explain the top-down security-analysis process and identify one question to investigate at each level.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Analyse two channels through which the scenario could affect Horizon's future cash flows or required return.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Evaluate the broker's prediction, including the difference between actual news and what investors already expected.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Explain how industry competition and Horizon's individual financial position could lead to a different conclusion from an economy-only analysis.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 2 - Reading financial statements together (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>The following simplified information describes Apex. Its accounts balance; there are no preferred shares. Figures are RM million.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Beginning of year</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">End of year</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">20</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Receivables</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inventory</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other current assets</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-current assets</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">120</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">115</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total assets</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>200</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>240</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Current liabilities</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">40</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-current liabilities</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total liabilities</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>120</strong></td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>160</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinary equity</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">80</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Annual sales were 300, cost of goods sold 180, EBIT 40, interest expense 10, tax 6, and net income 24. Cash flow from operations was 10. Assume all sales were on credit.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Distinguish the balance sheet, income statement and cash-flow statement by what each reports and its time frame. Verify the ending accounting equation.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate the beginning and ending current ratios and working capital; interpret their movement.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain why RM24 profit can coexist with RM10 operating cash flow. Use changes in working capital as a possible mechanism without claiming they fully reconcile the difference.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Identify two concerns and two follow-up checks before deciding that Apex is in financial difficulty.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 3 - Asset efficiency and liquidity (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Use Apex's figures from Question 2. A competitor's comparable annual receivables turnover is 10 times and inventory turnover is 6 times. Assume accounting policies and periods are comparable for this exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate Apex's receivables turnover, approximate average collection period using 365 days, and inventory turnover.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Calculate total asset turnover and explain the economic meaning of each turnover measure.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Compare receivables and inventory turnover with the competitor. Explain two plausible causes or consequences of the differences.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate the claim: \"A higher inventory turnover is always better.\" Identify information that could change the conclusion.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 4 - Leverage and profitability (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Continue with Apex's data. Recall EBIT = 40, interest expense = 10, sales = 300 and net income = 24. Total liabilities at year-end are 160 and ordinary equity is 80; beginning and ending total assets are 200 and 240, and ordinary equity is 80 at both dates.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [4] Calculate the year-end liabilities-to-equity ratio and times interest earned. Explain what each indicates.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [6] Calculate net profit margin, return on average assets and return on average equity.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Decompose ROE into net profit margin x asset turnover x equity multiplier using consistently averaged balances. Explain what leverage contributes to this identity.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate: \"A higher ROE proves management improved operations.\" Include a stress scenario and identify at least two additional checks.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 5 - Per-share measures and valuation signals (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Beacon has annual net income of RM25 million, preferred dividends of RM1 million, 12 million weighted-average ordinary shares, annual ordinary dividends of RM9.6 million, and ordinary book equity of RM96 million. Its current share price is RM30. For this exercise, assume the 12 million weighted-average shares also equal shares outstanding at the balance-sheet date. A forecast suggests earnings growth of 10% per year; compute PEG by entering growth as <strong>10</strong>, not 0.10.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate EPS, dividend per ordinary share and payout ratio.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [6] Calculate P/E, book value per ordinary share and P/B.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [3] Calculate PEG under the stated convention and identify one limitation.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate the claim: \"P/E above 10 and P/B above 1 prove the share is overpriced.\" Identify the additional evidence required before a recommendation.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 6 - Integrated decision and research plan (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Apex's shares trade at RM12; its estimated intrinsic value is RM15 per share, calculated using assumptions supplied by an analyst. Apex's sales grew 5% in the latest year while year-end receivables rose from RM30 million to RM50 million and inventory from RM30 million to RM60 million. Operating cash flow was RM10 million versus RM24 million of net income. Management proposes extra borrowing to fund expansion.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the percentage growth in receivables and inventory; compare both with sales growth.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Analyse what this pattern and the operating-cash-flow gap might indicate. Give plausible alternative explanations.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Evaluate the alleged undervaluation and explain how leverage and the valuation assumptions affect the conclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a prioritized research plan covering the economy, industry, financial statements and valuation. State a conditional decision and what new evidence would change it.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Full model answers and illustrative marking guidance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Answer 1 - Economic, industry and company analysis</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Top-down analysis [5].</strong> Economic analysis asks how growth, rates, inflation and exchange rates may affect firms. For Horizon, ask whether higher financing costs and slower customer spending are likely to reduce machinery demand. Industry analysis asks about machinery-sector competition, customer bargaining power, regulation and stage of growth. Company analysis examines Horizon's order backlog, margins, liquidity, debt terms and cash generation. These findings inform cash-flow and risk assumptions used to estimate value and compare it with the share price. <em>Marking: economy 1, industry 1, company 1, relevant questions 1, valuation connection 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Transmission [5].</strong> Higher rates can raise interest expense on floating-rate borrowing, lowering cash available to equity holders if all else is equal. Slower spending can prompt customers to delay major equipment purchases, reducing Horizon's volume and potentially margins. Exchange-rate movements could alter export revenue when translated into home currency and affect competitiveness, but the direction depends on currencies, pricing and costs. Rates may also alter the required return used to discount expected cash flows. <em>Marking: 2 marks for each of two explained channels and 1 for their valuation relevance or qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Prediction [5].</strong> The claim is too certain. Stock prices reflect investors' expectations and may adjust before economic weakness appears in reported data. If weakness is already priced in, or actual results are less bad than expected, the share could rise; worse-than-expected news could cause it to fall. Uncertainty, other news and changing required returns also matter. A weak economy alone does not establish next month's direction. <em>Marking: conditional judgment 1, expectations versus surprise 2, possible outcomes 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Industry and company [5].</strong> A firm with long contracts, a strong balance sheet and pricing power may withstand a slowdown better than a highly leveraged competitor with weak orders. Conversely, intense competition or customer concentration may worsen the effect. Compare margins, backlog, refinancing schedule, debt service and competitors before interpreting the economy-only forecast. An attractive business can still be a poor investment at an excessive price. <em>Marking: industry mechanism 2, company financial mechanism 2, price/valuation qualification 1.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 2 - Statements and liquidity</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Statements [5].</strong> The balance sheet reports assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time; the income statement reports revenues, expenses and profit over a period; the cash-flow statement reports cash generated or used in operating, investing and financing activities over a period. At year-end, $240=160+80$, so assets equal liabilities plus equity. <em>Marking: 1 each per statement, 1 for the time distinction, 1 for the equation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Liquidity [5].</strong> Beginning current assets are $20+30+30=80$; ending current assets are $15+50+60=125$.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Beginning current ratio}=80/40=2.00,\\quad \\text{ending current ratio}=125/80=1.5625\\approx1.56$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Beginning working capital}=80-40=40,\\quad \\text{ending working capital}=125-80=45$$</div>\n<p>The ratio fell despite an increase in working capital because current liabilities grew proportionately faster. There is a smaller relative short-term cushion; current assets include receivables and inventory that may not convert to cash promptly. <em>Marking: 2 ratios 2, working-capital figures 2, interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Profit versus cash [5].</strong> Net income is accrual-based, whereas operating cash flow records cash effects of operating activity. Sales booked on credit can increase receivables without immediate cash receipt; buying or producing additional inventory can absorb cash before sale. Noncash expenses, timing of payables and other working-capital changes can also affect the reconciliation. The stated data do not identify every reconciliation item, so they do not prove a particular cause for the full RM14 difference. <em>Marking: accrual versus cash 2, working-capital channels 2, limitation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Concerns and follow-up [5].</strong> Receivables rose by RM20 and inventory by RM30 while cash declined; the liquidity ratio also fell and operating cash flow is below profit. Investigate aged receivables and subsequent collections, inventory composition and obsolescence, liability due dates, credit facilities and seasonality, and the full cash-flow reconciliation. Growth or planned stocking may partly explain the changes. None alone proves distress. <em>Marking: 2 concerns 2, 2 relevant checks 2, balanced judgment 1.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 3 - Activity measures</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Receivables and inventory [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Average receivables}=(30+50)/2=40;\\quad \\text{turnover}=300/40=7.50\\text{ times}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Approximate collection period}=365/7.50=48.67\\text{ days}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Average inventory}=(30+60)/2=45;\\quad \\text{inventory turnover}=180/45=4.00\\text{ times}$$</div>\n<p>The collection estimate is an approximation; actual payment timing and seasonality may differ. <em>Marking: averages 2, turnovers 2, days 1, interpretation or units 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Asset turnover [4].</strong> Average assets are $(200+240)/2=220$ and total asset turnover is $300/220=1.36$ times. Receivables turnover relates credit sales to average outstanding customer balances; inventory turnover relates cost of sales to average inventory; asset turnover relates sales to the asset base. These are different activities and should not be interchanged. <em>Marking: calculation 2, meanings 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Comparison [5].</strong> Apex collects receivables about 7.5 times per year compared with the peer's 10 and turns inventory 4 times versus 6. Slower collection could mean looser credit, delayed customers or different seasonality; slower inventory turnover might reflect higher stocks, weak sales, preparation for growth or different product mix. Either can tie up cash and require funding. Examine aged balances, stock condition and comparable accounting before attributing a cause. <em>Marking: comparison 2, two causal explanations/consequences 2, qualification 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [5].</strong> Reject \"always.\" Excessively lean inventory may create stockouts, lost sales and supply-chain disruption; a high ratio can also reflect different purchasing practices or a temporarily small average denominator. Examine service levels, shortages, inventory quality, the product mix and peer definitions. A slower rate may be justified by a deliberate inventory buffer, although obsolescence can make it risky. <em>Marking: conclusion 1, trade-off 2, relevant evidence 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 4 - Profitability and leverage</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Leverage [4].</strong> Ending liabilities-to-equity $=160/80=2.00$ times. Times interest earned $=EBIT/\\text{interest}=40/10=4.00$ times. The first compares liabilities with equity under the stated convention; the second indicates EBIT is four times the current period's interest expense. Neither guarantees future solvency, and EBIT is not a direct cash-flow measure. <em>Marking: 1 per calculation, 1 per interpretation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Profitability [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Net margin}=24/300=8.00\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ROA=24/[(200+240)/2]=24/220=10.91\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ROE=24/[(80+80)/2]=24/80=30.00\\%$$</div>\n<p>These show net earnings per unit of sales, average assets and average ordinary equity respectively. <em>Marking: 2 marks each for correct formula, result and meaning.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Decomposition [5].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ROE=(24/300)\\times(300/220)\\times(220/80)=0.08\\times1.3636\\times2.75=30\\%$$</div>\n<p>The equity multiplier, 2.75, relates average assets to average equity. It captures how much assets are supported per unit of equity in this identity; financing and liabilities contribute to the gap. A higher multiplier can amplify equity returns, but debt cost and the effect of leverage on net income must also be considered. <em>Marking: components 3, identity 1, leverage interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [5].</strong> Higher ROE can arise from higher margin or turnover, but also from a higher equity multiplier or a smaller equity base. For instance, if EBIT falls to RM20 while interest remains RM10, coverage drops from 4 to 2 times before any further deterioration. Investigate margin and asset-turnover trends, debt maturity and interest terms, operating cash flow, and peers. Improved ROE does not establish improved operations or adequate compensation for increased risk. <em>Marking: counterargument 2, stress illustration 1, checks 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 5 - Equity ratios</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Earnings and distributions [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EPS=(25-1)/12=RM2.00$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$DPS=9.6/12=RM0.80;\\quad \\text{payout}=0.80/2.00=40\\%$$</div>\n<p>Preferred dividends are deducted because EPS measures income available to ordinary shareholders. <em>Marking: 2 each for EPS, DPS and payout.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Price measures [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P/E=30/2=15.00\\text{ times}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Book value/share}=96/12=RM8.00;\\quad P/B=30/8=3.75\\text{ times}$$</div>\n<p>P/E compares the share price to earnings per share for the specified period; P/B compares price with accounting equity per share. Neither ratio alone determines intrinsic value. <em>Marking: 2 each for correct calculation and meaning.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) PEG [3].</strong> $PEG=15/10=1.50$ under the explicitly stated percentage-point convention. It is sensitive to the growth estimate and the consistency of earnings and growth periods; a mechanical threshold is not proof of fair value. <em>Marking: computation 2, limitation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [5].</strong> Reject the claim. High multiples can reflect expected growth, risk, return on capital or accounting differences; low multiples can reflect poor prospects or risk. Investigate earnings quality, sustainable cash flows, expected growth, leverage, peer and industry comparability, book-value composition and the required return, then estimate value under transparent assumptions. A share can trade above book value without being overpriced. <em>Marking: conclusion 1, reasons multiples differ 2, additional evidence and valuation 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 6 - Integrated assessment</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Growth [5].</strong> Receivables growth $=(50-30)/30=66.67\\%$. Inventory growth $=(60-30)/30=100.00\\%$. Both substantially exceed 5% sales growth, indicating more working capital tied up relative to the stated sales trend. <em>Marking: 2 marks each for calculations, 1 for comparison.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Interpretation [5].</strong> Slow collection or expanding credit may explain receivables; inventory may be accumulating because of weak demand or stocking in anticipation of growth. These increases can absorb cash and help explain operating cash flow below net income. However, a deliberate expansion, seasonality or timing of cash collections and payments could also contribute. Obtain aging schedules, subsequent collections, stock aging, credit terms and the detailed cash-flow reconciliation; the pattern is a warning, not proof of manipulation or distress. <em>Marking: mechanisms 2, cash link 1, alternatives and checks 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Value and financing [5].</strong> RM15 estimated value is RM3 above the RM12 price, or $3/12=25\\%$ upside to that estimate before costs; it is an estimate, not a guaranteed gain. Check the analyst's cash-flow growth, working-capital needs, discount rate and financing assumptions. Borrowing may fund profitable expansion, but interest and repayment commitments increase financial risk and can change both expected cash flows and required return. If slower cash conversion persists or borrowing costs increase, the estimated value may fall below price. <em>Marking: comparison and calculation 2, estimate limitation 1, leverage and assumptions 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Research plan and conditional decision [5].</strong> First assess macroeconomic demand and rates relevant to Apex, then industry growth, competition and customer conditions. Review audited statements, receivables and inventory aging, operating cash conversion, borrowing terms and peer trends; reconstruct a valuation under base and adverse scenarios. Defer a firm buy recommendation until the working-capital increases and financing are explained and a conservative estimate still justifies the price for the investor's risk constraints. Strong subsequent collections and viable project returns could support buying; worsening collections or refinancing risk could justify avoiding it. <em>Marking: economic and industry checks 1, financial checks 1, valuation sensitivity 1, conditional decision 1, change-of-evidence trigger 1.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Short-answer revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><ol>\n<li>Why can the stock market fall even when a company reports higher earnings?</li>\n<li>How do an industry life cycle and a company's competitive position answer different questions?</li>\n<li>Explain how a profitable company can have weak cash flow from operations.</li>\n<li>Why is the current ratio more informative when combined with asset quality and liability maturity?</li>\n<li>Explain the difference between receivables turnover and inventory turnover.</li>\n<li>Why must an analyst state whether debt/equity includes all liabilities or only interest-bearing debt?</li>\n<li>How can leverage increase ROE without improving asset turnover or profit margin?</li>\n<li>Why do P/E and P/B ratios require comparison and interpretation rather than automatic buy/sell rules?</li>\n</ol></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Formula-selection map</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l7_dupont\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The DuPont tree with the notes&#x27; numbers: ROE splits into profitability, efficiency and leverage.</figcaption></figure></div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Formula under this paper's conventions</th>\n<th>What to check</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Short-term liquidity</td>\n<td>Current assets / current liabilities; working capital = current assets - current liabilities</td>\n<td>Asset quality, maturity and seasonality.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Collection and inventory</td>\n<td>Credit sales / average receivables; cost of goods sold / average inventory</td>\n<td>Use consistent periods and definitions; collection days approximately 365 / turnover.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operating efficiency</td>\n<td>Sales / average total assets</td>\n<td>Comparable asset intensity and accounting.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financing</td>\n<td>Total liabilities / ending ordinary equity; EBIT / interest expense</td>\n<td>Debt definition and ability to pay from cash flows.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Profitability</td>\n<td>Net income / sales, average assets or average ordinary equity</td>\n<td>Consistent period and appropriate numerator.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinary-share measures</td>\n<td>EPS = (net income - preferred dividends) / weighted-average shares; P/E = price / EPS</td>\n<td>Share count timing, earnings quality and comparability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Book and dividends</td>\n<td>Common book equity / ending shares; P/B = price / book value per share; payout = DPS / EPS</td>\n<td>Preferred claims, per-share basis and accounting conventions.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h4>Final integrated exercise</h4>\n<p>A company reports a record ROE and a share price below an analyst's estimated intrinsic value. At the same time its inventory, debt and receivables are growing much faster than sales. Build an argument both for and against buying the share. Then specify the evidence that would change your decision. A strong answer decomposes ROE, examines working-capital quality and borrowing capacity, challenges the valuation inputs and considers whether the potential reward compensates for risk; it does not equate either a warning signal or a positive valuation estimate with certainty.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Based on the learning goals and concepts in <code>pdf/</code> (Pearson, 2014). Scenarios, conventions and marking guidance are original. The source's slide 7-43 heading says ROA while the text and equity multiplier discuss ROE; the decomposition above identifies the correct measures and states its averaging convention.</p></div></details></div></details>");
